For any $\gamma\in\mathcal{X}_{\mathsf{BV}}(R)$, it holds that $\widetilde{\gamma}\in L^\infty(\mathbb{R}^d)$ and thus $\widetilde{\gamma}\in L^1_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbb{R}^d)$. Hence, since $\mathbb{R}^d$ is open, $V(\widetilde{\gamma};\mathbb{R}^d)$ is well-defined \cite[Definition~14.2, p.~460]{leoni2017first}. For a set $U\subseteq\mathbb{R}^d$, write $\Phi_U$ for the set of all $\phi\in C_c^{\infty}(U;\mathbb{R}^d)$ such that $\normtemp{\phi}_{L^\infty(U;\mathbb{R}^d)}\leq 1$. Then
\begin{align*}
    V(\widetilde{\gamma};\mathbb{R}^d) &=
    \sup_{\phi\in\Phi_{\mathbb{R}^d}} \sum_{i=1}^d\int_{\mathbb{R}^d}\widetilde{\gamma}(x)\partial_i\phi_i(x)\dd{x}\\
    &=
    \sup_{\phi\in\Phi_{\mathbb{R}^d}} \sum_{i=1}^d\biggl(\int_{\Omega}\widetilde{\gamma}(x)\partial_i\phi_i(x)\dd{x} + \int_{\mathbb{R}^d\setminus\Omega}\partial_i\phi_i(x)\dd{x}\biggr)\\
    &= \sup_{\phi\in\Phi_{\mathbb{R}^d}} \sum_{i=1}^d \biggl(\int_{\Omega} \gamma(x)\partial_i\phi_i(x)\dd{x} + \biggl[\int_{\mathbb{R}^d}\partial_i\phi_i(x)\dd{x} - \int_{\Omega}\partial_i\phi_i(x)\dd{x}\biggr]\biggr)\\
    &= \sup_{\phi\in\Phi_{\mathbb{R}^d}} \sum_{i=1}^d \biggl(\int_{\Omega} \bigl(\gamma(x)-1\bigr)\partial_i\phi_i(x)\dd{x}\biggr)\,.
\end{align*}
In the third line, the second integral equals zero by the divergence theorem due to the compact support of $\phi\in\Phi_{\mathbb{R}^d}$. It remains to show that
\begin{align*}
    \sup_{\phi\in\Phi_{\mathbb{R}^d}} \sum_{i=1}^d \biggl(\int_{\Omega} \bigl(\gamma(x)-1\bigr)\partial_i\phi_i(x)\dd{x}\biggr) = \sup_{\phi\in\Phi_{\Omega}} \sum_{i=1}^d \biggl(\int_{\Omega} \gamma(x)\partial_i\phi_i(x)\dd{x}\biggr)
\end{align*}
because the rightmost quantity equals $V(\gamma;\Omega)$.    
If $\phi\in \Phi_\Omega$, then $\sup_{x\in\mathbb{R}^d}\abstemp{\phi(x)}=\sup_{x\in\Omega}\abstemp{\phi(x)}\leq 1$ because $\phi$ is compactly supported in $\Omega$. Since $\Omega\subseteq\mathbb{R}^d$, it holds that $\phi\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R}^d;\mathbb{R}^d)$ as well. We have implicitly extended $\phi$ by zero to all of $\mathbb{R}^d$. This implies that $\Phi_\Omega\subseteq \Phi_{\mathbb{R}^d}$ and thus
\begin{align*}
    V(\widetilde{\gamma};\mathbb{R}^d)&\geq \sup_{\phi\in\Phi_{\Omega}} \sum_{i=1}^d \biggl(\int_{\Omega} \bigl(\gamma(x)-1\bigr)\partial_i\phi_i(x)\dd{x}\biggr)\\
    &= V(\gamma;\Omega)\,.
\end{align*}
To achieve the final equality, we use the fact that $\mathrm{supp}(\phi)\subset\Omega$ and apply the divergence theorem.

To show the upper bound, fix any $\phi\in\Phi_{\mathbb{R}^d}$ and $i\in\{1,\ldots, d\}$. Let $\rchi_U$ denote the indicator function of a set $U\subseteq\mathbb{R}^d$. Since $\gamma=\mathds{1}$ on $\Omega\setminus\Omega'$ and $\widetilde{\gamma}=\mathds{1}$ on $\mathbb{R}^d\setminus\Omega$ from \eqref{eqn:compact_support_set} and \eqref{eqn:conductivity_set_prelim}, it follows that $\widetilde{\gamma}=\mathds{1}$ on $\mathbb{R}^d\setminus\Omega'$, all in the a.e. sense. Then
\begin{align*}
    \int_{\Omega} \bigl(\gamma(x)-1\bigr)\partial_i\phi_i(x)\dd{x}&=\int_{\mathbb{R}^d}\rchi_{\Omega'}(x) \bigl(\widetilde{\gamma}(x)-1\bigr)\partial_i\phi_i(x)\dd{x}\\
    &=\int_{\mathbb{R}^d}\rchi_{\Omega'}(x) \bigl(\widetilde{\gamma}(x)-1\bigr)\partial_i\xi_i(x)\dd{x}\\
    &=\int_{\Omega'} \bigl(\gamma(x)-1\bigr)\partial_i\xi_i(x)\dd{x}
\end{align*}
for any smooth enough $\xi\colon\mathbb{R}^d\to\mathbb{R}^d$ such that $\xi_i=\phi_i$ on $\Omega'$ for each $i$. By compact containment, we can find another domain $\Omega''\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ such that $\overline{\Omega'}\subset\Omega''\subset\overline{\Omega''}\subset\Omega$.
Let $\varrho\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R}^d;\mathbb{R})$ be a smooth cutoff function such that $\varrho \equiv 1$ on $\overline{\Omega'}$ and $\varrho\equiv 0$ on $\mathbb{R}^d\setminus \Omega''$. Define $\xi\coloneqq \varrho\phi$ in the pointwise sense. Then $\xi$ belongs to $C_c^\infty(\Omega;\mathbb{R}^d)$ because smoothness is preserved under pointwise multiplication and $\mathrm{supp}(\xi)\subseteq \mathrm{supp}(\varrho)\subseteq \Omega''\subset\Omega $. Furthermore, $\sup_{x\in\Omega}\abstemp{\xi(x)}\leq \sup_{x\in\mathbb{R}^d}\abstemp{\xi(x)}\leq 1$ because both $\sup_{x\in\mathbb{R}^d}\abstemp{\varrho(x)}$ and $\sup_{x\in\mathbb{R}^d}\abstemp{\phi(x)}$ are bounded above by one. We deduce that $\xi\in\Phi_{\Omega}$. Summing the previous display and taking the supremum on the right yields
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{i=1}^d \biggl(\int_{\Omega} \bigl(\gamma(x)-1\bigr)\partial_i\phi_i(x)\dd{x}\biggr)\leq \sup_{\xi'\in\Phi_\Omega}\sum_{i=1}^d \biggl(\int_{\Omega'} \bigl(\gamma(x)-1\bigr)\partial_i\xi'_i(x)\dd{x}\biggr)\,.
\end{align*}
By replacing $\Omega'$ with $\Omega$ in the integral, taking the supremum over $\phi\in\Phi_{\mathbb{R}^d}$ on the left-hand side of the preceding inequality, and applying the divergence theorem a final time, the desired result $V(\widetilde{\gamma};\mathbb{R}^d)\leq V(\gamma;\Omega)$ follows.

